var e="# Limits\n\nEvery limit below is enforced by the server. Most come back **in the response**, so\nyou find out from the API rather than from a hung tab or a surprise bill.\n\n## Quotas\n\n| Limit | Value | Enforced |\n|---|---|---|\n| Projects per account | **2** | Checked inside the transaction that creates a project. |\n| Database size | **256 MB** per project | Before every write; SQLite itself refuses page growth past the ceiling. |\n| Storage | **256 MB** per project | On upload, against usage plus declared size. |\n| Object size | Bucket's `max_object_size_bytes`, or 256 MB | On upload. |\n\nAutomatic database backups are stored **separately** and do **not** count\nagainst a project's storage quota — that quota is for the objects you serve. See\n[Backups](/docs/backups).\n\n### Projects per account\n\nTwo. `POST /api/projects` returns `429 quota_exceeded` for a third.\n\nThe check happens **inside the transaction** that creates the project, not before it. Two simultaneous requests cannot both pass a check-then-create and leave you with three projects.\n\n### Database size\n\n256 MB, measured as `(page_count - freelist_count) * page_size` — the data pages, so space freed by a DELETE starts counting as free immediately.\n\nThe check runs **before** the write, and SQLite refuses the page growth that would cross the ceiling, so a write that would overrun is refused; it is not partially applied and it is not silently truncated.\n\nDatabase does not auto-shrink when data is deleted. Physical space remains until you run `moogo db vacuum` or `POST /maintenance` (see [SQL API](/docs/sql-api)). Reclaim space regularly after large deletes.\n\n### Result size\n\nOne read response carries at most **16 MB** of row data. The row cap of 1000 bounds how many rows come back; this bounds how many bytes they add up to — a thousand rows of a wide `BLOB` column would otherwise be a response held twice in memory, once as rows and once as JSON.\n\nA single value larger than the project's own size limit is refused before SQLite materializes it, so `SELECT zeroblob(n)` cannot ask for more bytes than the file would ever hold.\n\n\n\n### Binary size & serverless considerations\n\nWhen uploading large files or using image compression (e.g., `sharp` in Node.js, `Pillow` in Python), keep in mind:\n\n- **Per-object cap**: Each bucket has `max_object_size_bytes` (default: no cap, or configured by admin). Objects over this cap are refused.\n- **Project quota**: 256 MB total across all buckets in a project. Exceeding this returns `quota_exceeded` (429).\n- **Edge/serverless memory**: Functions that stream uploads should respect the per-request cap (1 MB for JSON endpoints). For larger files, use the direct storage credential flow (`/p/{id}/bucket/*`) rather than JSON bodies.\n- **Memory during compression**: `sharp` and similar tools allocate memory proportional to image dimensions. Ensure your function's memory limit (e.g., 256 MB on Vercel, 128 MB on Netlify) is sufficient for the source image + compressed output.\n\n**Tip**: Compress/resize images client-side or in a build step before uploading to Moogo, rather than in a serverless function that has limited memory.\n## Rate limits\n\n\n### Edge Runtime & sharp di Next.js\n\n**Catatan Penting**: Perintah `sharp` hanya bekerja di runtime **Node.js**, bukan di **Edge Runtime**. Jika aplikasi Anda menggunakan `runtime: \"edge\"` di `next.config.js`, perintah `sharp` akan gagal dijalankan.\n\n**Saran**:\n- **Server Component / Action**: Gunakan `sharp` di sini (runtime Node.js) untuk compress/resize gambar sebelum upload.\n- **Browser / Edge Runtime**: Kompress gambar di sisi klien (browser) menggunakan library seperti [`sharp-mobile`](https://github.com/sharpjs/sharp-mobile) atau library kompresi lain, atau unggah gambara asli dan lakukan kompressi di server.\n\n**Alur kerja yang disarankan**:\n1. **Build Step** (direkomendasikan): Kompress gambar saat build/deploy menggunakan `sharp`, lalu unggah file yang sudah dikompress ke Moogo.\n2. **Server Component**: Jalankan `sharp` di Server Component/Action (runtime Node.js).\n3. **Browser-only**: Gunakan library kompresi di sisi klien, atau unggah asli dan beri peringatan kepada pengguna tentang ukuran file.\n\n### Binary size & serverless considerations\n\nWhen uploading large files or using image compression (e.g., `sharp` in Node.js, `Pillow` in Python), keep in mind:\n\n- **Per object**: the bucket's `max_object_size_bytes`, or 256 MB\n- **Project quota**: 256 MB total across all buckets\n- **Edge/serverless memory**: Functions that stream uploads should respect the per-request cap (1 MB for JSON endpoints). For larger files, use the direct storage credential flow (`/p/{id}/bucket/*`) rather than JSON bodies.\n- **Memory during compression**: `sharp` and similar tools allocate memory proportional to image dimensions. Ensure your function's memory limit (e.g., 256 MB on Vercel, 128 MB on Netlify) is sufficient for the source image + compressed output.\n\n**Tip**: Compress/resize images client-side or in a build step before uploading to Moogo, rather than in a serverless function that has limited memory.\n\nEvery response includes standard rate-limit headers so clients can see their\nbudget without hitting a 429 first.\n\n| Header | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `X-RateLimit-Limit` | Maximum requests allowed in the current window |\n| `X-RateLimit-Remaining` | Requests remaining in the current window |\n| `X-RateLimit-Reset` | Unix timestamp (seconds) when the allowance refills to the limit |\n\n| Endpoint group | Limit | Window | Keyed by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Sign-in (email/password: `/auth/register`, `/auth/login`, `/auth/forgot-password`, `/auth/reset-password`, `/auth/verify-email`, `/auth/resend-verification`) | 10 | 1 minute | Client IP (trusted proxy) |\n| OAuth (`/auth/google`, `/auth/google/callback`, `/auth/setup`) | 30 | 1 minute | Client IP (trusted proxy) |\n| SQL reads (`/query`) | 300 | 1 minute | Project ID |\n| SQL writes (`/exec`, `/transaction`) | 300 | 1 minute | Project ID |\n| Object storage | 300 | 1 minute | Client IP (trusted proxy) |\n| Updates (`/maintenance`, `/exec` DDL) | 60 | 1 minute | Project ID |\n\nWhen a limit is exceeded, the response is `429 Too Many Requests` with\n`error.code = \"rate_limited\"`, a `Retry-After` header (seconds), and the\nstandard rate-limit headers above. Use `X-RateLimit-Reset` to schedule your\nretry precisely instead of guessing.\n\n> **Note:** The email/password credential limiter is shared across all six unauthenticated\n> endpoints (`/auth/register`, `/auth/login`, `/auth/forgot-password`, `/auth/reset-password`, `/auth/verify-email`, `/auth/resend-verification`) so the allowance cannot be multiplied by moving between endpoints.\n> \n> OAuth endpoints have a separate, looser limiter (30/min) since they are GET-based and the callback does lighter work than a bcrypt compare, but still caps enumeration/abuse.";export{e as default};